        "date": "2025-12-22T09:56:01Z"
      },
      "message": "Reference Model: Refine the rule analyzer and matching logics\n\nFrom the IOPMP spec, an entry qualifies as a matching entry if:\n- Its region covers any byte of the transaction,\n- It is associated with the RRID carried by the transaction; and\n- It holds the highest priority among entries that meet the previous\n  criteria.\n\nTechnically speaking, a \"matching entry\" doesn't mean that the entry\nalso grants transaction permission. The model needs to distinguish\nbetween the \"maching entry without granting permission\" and \"matching\nentry with granting permission\".\n\nThis commit splits standard error types from iopmpMatchStatus_t as\niopmpErrorType_t, and defines five status, including partial matching\nand whether the matching entry grants transaction permission or not.\n\nNow, iopmpMatchAddr() just check the address range of the entry.\nWhether the entry \"fully matches\", \"partially matches\" or \"doesn't\nmatch\" the bytes of the transaction, regardless of the entry is a\npriority entry or not.\n\nThe iopmpCheckPerms() checks permission bits of the entry which \"fully\nmatches\" all bytes of the transaction, and simply returns whether the\nentry \"grants\" or \"doesn't grant\" the transaction requested permission.\n\nThe final judgements are performed in iopmp_validate_access(). This\nfunction gets the result from iopmpRuleAnalyzer(), determining if the\nentry is non-priority entry or not, and judges if the transaction is\npassed or the searching needs to be continued or stopped with an error\ncapture.",
